-- search_candidates: 0 insert(s), 2 update(s)
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "disposition" = 'ADMITTED', "notes" = ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01003): Retrieved via Crossref. The staged description called it a second independent perceived-difficulty study on running slope AND cross slope, distance-conditioned -- that half holds. WHAT THE HYPOTHESIS GOT WRONG: it did not say the 23 participants WALKED at their preferred pace, so they are ambulatory people with vision or mobility limitations, NOT wheelchair users. Graded PROXY for MOB on that basis. Full byline is Longmuir, Freeland, Fitzgerald, Yamada and Axelson, not Longmuir alone as the Consensus record implied. T3 (clinical + lower_control): a pilot field study with no control condition, so it cannot lift parameter 3 off proxy.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 107;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "disposition" = 'ADMITTED', "notes" = ' || RESOLVED 2026-09-18 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01004): Retrieved via Crossref. The staged description held: five slope levels (1:6, 1:8, 1:10, 1:12, 1:14) crossed with three rise heights (15, 30, 45 cm), with perceived discomfort alongside EMG and pulse. WHAT IT ADDS BEYOND THE HYPOTHESIS: the paper states 1:8 is recommendable when rise is LOW and installation space insufficient -- a second anchoring-tier stated value, pointing the opposite way from REF-01002 7 percent, and reconciling with it through conditions rather than contradicting (one conditions on distance, one on rise). Also reports no significant difference between 1:10 and 1:12. Published in HFES Proceedings 54(9):698-702; a Korean-language twin exists at 10.5143/jesk.2010.29.4.681 and is the same study in a second venue.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 108;